        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The start of the NASA 5 mission was marked by the launch of STS-84, in May 1997.  STS-84 was the sixth docking mission between the U.S. Space Shuttle and Russia's Mir Space Station.   Astronaut Michael Foale joined the Mir 23 crew, replacing Jerry Linenger as the fifth U.S. long-duration astronaut on the Mir.  During STS-84's docked phase, water, food and other resupply items, along with Mir 23/NASA 5 research hardware and equipment, was transferred from Atlantis to the Mir.  Atlantis returned to Earth with Russian, European Space Agency, and U.S. science samples and hardware, and Jerry Linenger, who had been on Mir since January 1997.  On August 5, 1997, the Mir 24 crew launched to join Foale on Mir and replace the Mir 23 crew.  <P>On June 25, 1997, during a docking maneuver, the unmanned Progress resupply ship collided with the Mir complex, damaging the Mir module called Spektr.  Spektr experienced a depressurization and was sealed off from the rest of the Mir station by the crew.  Following this unfortunate incident, onboard activities focused on preserving as much science as possible.  The Mir crew conducted repairs to parts of the space station.  Some science hardware and research samples were lost, because they were inside the now inaccessible Spektr module.  Partial loss of station electrical power also impacted the schedule and performance of life sciences investigations during the mission.<P>Human Life Sciences research during the NASA 5 mission included studies on the adaptation of crewmembers to weightlessness in terms of skeletal muscle and bone changes, coordination and balance, psychological interactions, immune system function and metabolism.  In addition, environmental factors were assessed, including air and water quality, surface microbiology, and crew microbiology.  These investigations continued characterizing the integrated human response to extended periods of time in space.<P>Prolonged exposure to microgravity provides an ideal opportunity to determine the role that gravity plays in cell regulation and how this affects the development and growth of organisms.  Fundamental Biology research continued investigations to study the effect of the space environment on the biological systems of plants.  Specifically, wheat plants continued to be successfully grown in the Svet greenhouse.  Research was also conducted on beetles to determine how microgravity affects the insect's biological, or circadian, rhythms.  Both the beetles and the greenhouse were maintained during the mission, although a loss of some lighting was experienced.  Other investigations in this discipline characterized the radiation environment inside the Mir space station.<P>Commercially initiated research in the Advanced Technology discipline was aimed at evaluating new technologies and techniques using the Mir space station and the Shuttle as unique testing environments.  A better understanding of the characteristics of superconductors, protein crystal growth and the development of biological and chemical systems through fluid processing in reduced gravity will lead to an enhanced technology base for the design and operation of the International Space Station.  The knowledge gained also has the potential to significantly improve aspects of commercial processing here on Earth.<P>One group of experiments specifically prepared NASA for building and operating the International Space Station.  These investigations consisted of several technology demonstrations associated with human factors and maintenance of crew health and safety aboard the station.  To improve the design and operation of the International Space Station, information on the Mir interior and exterior environments was gathered and the station's structure and design was then evaluated.  The investigations studied docked configuration stability, radio frequency interference, the external Mir environment, radiation monitoring and water microbiological monitoring.<P>Microgravity research has the general goal of advancing scientific understanding through research in biotechnology, fluid physics, combustion science and materials science.  Microgravity research included the study of protein crystal growth and characterization of the effect of gravity on alloys.  The ambient acceleration and vibration environment of Mir was also characterized to support future research programs.<P>To better understand our Earth and solar system, research in ocean biochemistry, land surface hydrology, and meteorology was also performed.  Observation and documentation of transient natural and human-induced changes was accomplished with the use of hand-held photography.  Earth observation and photography from Mir allows for documentation of atmospheric conditions, ecological changes and seasonal changes over long time periods, as well as during ephemeral events.  In addition, widow surveys were performed to document the condition and degradation of the Mir window surfaces used for Earth observations.<P>Nearly all Shuttle-Mir investigations were joint efforts between the U.S. and Russia, with at least one researcher involved from each country.  In addition to the benefits of international cooperation, the NASA 5 mission added to the understanding of many scientific areas, including the effects of long-duration space flight on living organisms.  Despite the setbacks experienced during the NASA 5 mission, the NASA 5 and subsequent NASA 6 crews and ground-based teams made necessary repairs to the Mir and were well on the way to re-establishing a challenging science research program to continue the study of life sciences in space.<END>
